[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Chaos Physics, Collision Channels, and Sweep Queries in Unreal Engine"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unreal Engine's Chaos Physics system provides a robust framework for handling rigid body dynamics, complex collision filtering, and precise spatial queries. In this tutorial, we will explore how to configure Chaos collision channels and use sweep queries to detect geometry before movement occurs."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Understanding Chaos Collision Channels"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Collision channels in Unreal Engine determine how objects interact with one another during physics simulations and traces. By default, the engine provides standard channels like WorldStatic, WorldDynamic, and Pawn, but custom projects often require dedicated channels for gameplay mechanics."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Object Channels: Define what an actor physically is (e.g., Player, Enemy, Projectile)."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Trace Channels: Define how specific queries interact with the world (e.g., Visibility, Camera, MeleeSweep)."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Responses: Set each channel to Ignore, Overlap, or Block based on design requirements."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Implementing Sweep Queries in C++"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unlike standard raycasts, sweep queries project a geometric shape (such as a sphere, box, or capsule) along a vector. This is essential for preventing tunneling and implementing accurate melee hitboxes or character movement checks."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"bool AMyCharacter::PerformMeleeSweep(FHitResult& OutHit, FVector StartPos, FVector EndPos) {
    FCollisionShape MyColShape = FCollisionShape::MakeSphere(50.0f);
    FCollisionQueryParams QueryParams;
    QueryParams.AddIgnoredActor(this);

    return GetWorld()->SweepSingleByChannel(
        OutHit,
        StartPos,
        EndPos,
        FQuat::Identity,
        ECC_GameTraceChannel1,
        MyColShape,
        QueryParams
    );
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Best Practices for Chaos Performance"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use simple collision primitives (boxes, spheres, capsules) instead of complex-as-simple triangle meshes whenever possible."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Limit the frequency of sweep queries, especially multi-sweeps, to maintain high frame rates on tick functions."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Leverage object type filtering in query parameters to avoid unnecessary overlap checks against irrelevant actors."}]
